1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:05,020
This microscopic device hides a weapon
that can trigger a cell to destroy itself.

2
00:00:05,590 --> 00:00:08,080
In acidic surroundings, it opens.

3
00:00:08,850 --> 00:00:11,370
Now imagine choosing an
attack like this by testing

4
00:00:11,410 --> 00:00:13,860
it on a digital version
of your tumor first.

5
00:00:14,230 --> 00:00:16,780
That complete system is a possible future.

6
00:00:17,070 --> 00:00:19,040
AI has not cured all cancer.

7
00:00:19,350 --> 00:00:22,000
Researchers are already
building its separate parts.

8
00:00:22,804 --> 00:00:24,554
First, build the copy.

9
00:00:24,964 --> 00:00:27,294
Researchers are developing digital twins:

10
00:00:27,544 --> 00:00:30,784
mathematical models of particular
tumors, built from measurements

11
00:00:30,824 --> 00:00:34,334
such as medical scans and updated
as new information arrives.

12
00:00:34,604 --> 00:00:36,604
The goal is to compare possible treatments

13
00:00:36,644 --> 00:00:38,874
before choosing what
to try in the patient.

14
00:00:39,304 --> 00:00:41,374
One simulated tumor shrinks.

15
00:00:41,544 --> 00:00:43,214
Another barely changes.

16
00:00:43,424 --> 00:00:45,794
A third shrinks—then returns.

17
00:00:46,184 --> 00:00:48,354
The model is searching for a better plan.

18
00:00:48,524 --> 00:00:50,354
It is not a perfect copy of you,

19
00:00:50,404 --> 00:00:53,574
and a simulated success still
needs real-world testing.

20
00:00:53,864 --> 00:00:56,874
But it changes the question
from “What usually works?”

21
00:00:56,924 --> 00:00:59,134
to “What might work for this tumor?”

22
00:00:59,870 --> 00:01:02,006
Next, give the attack an address.

23
00:01:02,396 --> 00:01:04,506
Cancer grows from your own cells.

24
00:01:04,796 --> 00:01:07,966
A powerful drug is dangerous
if it reaches the wrong tissue.

25
00:01:08,396 --> 00:01:10,716
Some treatments already
attach a drug payload to

26
00:01:10,756 --> 00:01:13,666
an antibody that binds a
particular surface target.

27
00:01:13,876 --> 00:01:15,706
The cell takes the package inside.

28
00:01:15,976 --> 00:01:20,166
In certain designs, enzymes cut its
molecular linker, releasing the drug.

29
00:01:20,456 --> 00:01:23,246
The cell’s own machinery
helps open the delivery.

30
00:01:23,596 --> 00:01:27,966
These are antibody-drug conjugates, and
some are already used in cancer care.

31
00:01:28,236 --> 00:01:30,386
They can still cause serious side effects;

32
00:01:30,536 --> 00:01:33,186
a molecular address is
not perfect protection.

33
00:01:33,476 --> 00:01:36,006
AI could help develop
better targeting components.

34
00:01:36,296 --> 00:01:39,316
Researchers are already
testing AI-designed proteins as

35
00:01:39,356 --> 00:01:42,526
recognition parts in experimental
immune-cell therapies.

36
00:01:43,360 --> 00:01:46,788
But what if the attack stayed hidden
until its surroundings changed?

37
00:01:47,138 --> 00:01:48,988
Return to the device from the opening.

38
00:01:49,518 --> 00:01:52,928
Researchers at Karolinska
built a switch from folded DNA.

39
00:01:53,158 --> 00:01:55,258
It hides a pattern of
peptides that can bring

40
00:01:55,298 --> 00:01:57,788
death receptors together
on a cell’s surface.

41
00:01:58,058 --> 00:02:01,388
Under acidic conditions, the
structure exposes that pattern,

42
00:02:01,458 --> 00:02:04,068
helping trigger the cell’s
self-destruct process.

43
00:02:04,418 --> 00:02:08,888
They tested it in mice with breast cancer
tumors and reported reduced tumor growth.

44
00:02:09,178 --> 00:02:10,888
That is a real experiment.

45
00:02:11,078 --> 00:02:13,538
It is not a human cure,
and this particular

46
00:02:13,578 --> 00:02:16,608
nanodevice was not presented
as an AI invention.

47
00:02:16,978 --> 00:02:20,178
The future opportunity is to combine
better prediction with better

48
00:02:20,218 --> 00:02:24,408
biological engineering—if experiments
show those pieces work together.

49
00:02:25,330 --> 00:02:28,112
Then one cell threatens the entire plan.

50
00:02:28,702 --> 00:02:29,732
It survives.

51
00:02:30,502 --> 00:02:32,712
Tumors contain different groups of cells.

52
00:02:32,982 --> 00:02:37,512
A treatment can destroy sensitive ones
while resistant cells remain and multiply.

53
00:02:37,842 --> 00:02:41,192
So the plan cannot end when
the biggest mass gets smaller.

54
00:02:41,602 --> 00:02:44,062
Researchers have built AI
tools that predict drug

55
00:02:44,102 --> 00:02:47,332
response and resistance from
individual tumor-cell data.

56
00:02:47,602 --> 00:02:50,662
The longer-term ambition is to
help doctors anticipate escape

57
00:02:50,702 --> 00:02:53,732
routes and update treatment
as new measurements arrive.

58
00:02:54,730 --> 00:02:56,930
A digital twin that
learns from the disease.

59
00:02:57,120 --> 00:02:59,230
A treatment aimed at a vulnerability.

60
00:02:59,520 --> 00:03:02,890
A delivery system that controls
when its payload becomes active.

61
00:03:03,200 --> 00:03:05,530
Those technologies are
at different stages.

62
00:03:05,700 --> 00:03:07,610
They are not one finished machine,

63
00:03:07,680 --> 00:03:10,070
and different cancers will
need different approaches.

64
00:03:10,340 --> 00:03:12,530
A model can miss a resistant pocket.

65
00:03:12,720 --> 00:03:14,670
A carrier can reach healthy tissue.

66
00:03:14,840 --> 00:03:17,690
An apparent victory can
disappear at the next scan.

67
00:03:18,020 --> 00:03:22,430
That is why the final test is not whether
the tumor vanishes on a computer screen.

68
00:03:22,640 --> 00:03:27,070
It is whether people live longer, with
less harm, and the cancer stays away.

69
00:03:27,500 --> 00:03:30,410
The cell in our opening had
a self-destruct mechanism.

70
00:03:30,680 --> 00:03:34,730
The challenge is reaching the right
cell—and leaving it no way back.
